            <!-- VIEW: OVERVIEW -->
            <section id="view-overview" class="view-section active block">
                <header class="mb-8 border-b-2 border-xhosa-sand pb-4">
                    <h2 class="text-4xl font-serif text-xhosa-earth font-bold mb-3">Prologue: The Xhosa and their Environment</h2>
                    <p class="text-lg text-gray-700">An exhaustive analysis of the geographical, historiographical, and environmental foundations that shaped the early Xhosa nation prior to 1835.</p>
                </header>

                <div class="space-y-4">
                    <!-- Accordion 1 -->
                    <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-lg bg-white shadow-sm">
                        <button class="accordion-toggle w-full px-6 py-4 text-left font-semibold text-xhosa-earth hover:bg-xhosa-sand flex justify-between items-center" onclick="toggleAccordion(this)">
                            1. Geographical Environment & Settlement Patterns
                            <span class="text-xl">&#43;</span>
                        </button>
                        <div class="accordion-content px-6 bg-white">
                            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 py-4 space-y-2 text-gray-700">
                                <li><strong>Location:</strong> Historically situated between the Drakensberg mountains, the Indian Ocean, the Great Kei River, and stretching towards the Sundays River.</li>
                                <li><strong>Topography:</strong> Characterized by parallel river valleys running from the mountains to the sea, creating natural geographical boundaries and segmenting settlement.</li>
                                <li><strong>Rainfall and Climate:</strong> Reliable summer rainfall on the coastal terraces supported both pastoralism and agriculture, contrasting with the arid interior.</li>
                                <li><strong>Vegetation:</strong> Sweetveld (highly nutritious year-round) in the valleys, sourveld (nutritious only in spring/summer) on the ridges, necessitating seasonal cattle movement (transhumance).</li>
                                <li><strong>Settlement Structure:</strong>
                                    <ul class="list-[circle] pl-6 mt-1 space-y-1">
                                        <li>Dispersed homesteads (imizi) rather than concentrated villages.</li>
                                        <li>Driven by ecological necessity: avoiding overgrazing and exhaustion of water supplies.</li>
                                        <li>Each umzi was economically self-sufficient, centered around a cattle enclosure (ubuhlanti).</li>
                                    </ul>
                                </li>
                                <li><strong>Ecological Crisis:</strong> As expansion westward was halted by colonial frontiers, population density increased, leading to environmental stress and internal friction.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Accordion 2 -->
                    <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-lg bg-white shadow-sm">
                        <button class="accordion-toggle w-full px-6 py-4 text-left font-semibold text-xhosa-earth hover:bg-xhosa-sand flex justify-between items-center" onclick="toggleAccordion(this)">
                            2. Historiographical Context
                            <span class="text-xl">&#43;</span>
                        </button>
                        <div class="accordion-content px-6 bg-white">
                            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 py-4 space-y-2 text-gray-700">
                                <li><strong>Nature of Sources:</strong> Heavy reliance on oral traditions (amaBali) synthesized with early colonial written records (missionaries, travelers, officials).</li>
                                <li><strong>Bias in Written Records:</strong> Colonial sources often misunderstood Xhosa political structures, assuming despotic power where power was actually decentralized and consensual.</li>
                                <li><strong>Oral Tradition Dynamics:</strong> Traditions are not static; they serve as charters for present political relationships. The myth of Tshawe, for example, justifies the royal lineage's dominance.</li>
                                <li><strong>The "Hammond-Tooke Hypothesis":</strong> Peires argues against earlier anthropological models that suggested a rigid, purely kinship-based segmentation, proposing a more dynamic political process involving assimilation and power consolidation.</li>
                                <li><strong>Synthesis Approach:</strong> Peires combines structural anthropology with historical chronology to reconstruct a living, changing society rather than a static "traditional" baseline.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- VIEW: LINEAGE -->
            <section id="view-lineage" class="view-section hidden">
                <header class="mb-8 border-b-2 border-xhosa-sand pb-4">
                    <h2 class="text-4xl font-serif text-xhosa-earth font-bold mb-3">The Birth of the Nation & Royal Lineage</h2>
                    <p class="text-lg text-gray-700">An exhaustive hierarchical breakdown of the Tshawe royal lineage and the structural segmentation of the Xhosa people.</p>
                </header>

                <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200 mb-8">
                    <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-xhosa-clay mb-4">Interactive Lineage Tree (The Tshawe Dynasty)</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4 italic">Click on a chief to expand their sub-lineage and historical details.</p>
                    
                    <ul class="pl-0 space-y-2" id="lineage-root">
                        <!-- JS populated lineage -->
                    </ul>
                </div>
                
                <div class="space-y-4">
                    <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-lg bg-white shadow-sm">
                        <button class="accordion-toggle w-full px-6 py-4 text-left font-semibold text-xhosa-earth hover:bg-xhosa-sand flex justify-between items-center" onclick="toggleAccordion(this)">
                            Exhaustive Details: The Tshawe Conquest & Nation Building
                            <span class="text-xl">&#43;</span>
                        </button>
                        <div class="accordion-content px-6 bg-white">
                            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 py-4 space-y-2 text-gray-700">
                                <li><strong>The Myth of Origin:</strong> Xhosa identity is traced back to a mythical founder, Xhosa, but political unity was forged by Tshawe.</li>
                                <li><strong>The Tshawe Usurpation:</strong> Tshawe, a junior son, overthrew his senior brother Cirha with the help of the amaRudulu clan, establishing the amaTshawe as the royal clan.</li>
                                <li><strong>Mechanism of Expansion (Ukukhonza):</strong>
                                    <ul class="list-[circle] pl-6 mt-1 space-y-1">
                                        <li>The Tshawe did not conquer solely by force. They incorporated alien groups (Khoisan and other Bantu-speakers) through 'ukukhonza' (clientage/subordination).</li>
                                        <li>Incorporated groups retained internal autonomy but paid tribute and recognized Tshawe paramountcy.</li>
                                    </ul>
                                </li>
                                <li><strong>The Great Split (Reign of Phalo):</strong>
                                    <ul class="list-[circle] pl-6 mt-1 space-y-1">
                                        <li>Phalo married two women of equal rank simultaneously.</li>
                                        <li>To avoid civil war, the council created two 'Great Houses': The Great House (Gcaleka) and the Right-Hand House (Rharhabe).</li>
                                        <li>This institutionalized a structural division. The Gcaleka remained paramount in rank (east of the Kei), but the Rharhabe (west of the Kei) became politically independent.</li>
                                    </ul>
                                </li>
                                <li><strong>Subsequent Fissions:</strong> The Rharhabe further divided between Ngqika (Great House) and Ndlambe (Regent/uncle who refused to cede power), defining the frontier dynamics of the early 19th century.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- VIEW: POLITICAL -->
            <section id="view-political" class="view-section hidden">
                <header class="mb-8 border-b-2 border-xhosa-sand pb-4">
                    <h2 class="text-4xl font-serif text-xhosa-earth font-bold mb-3">Political System (Reign of Phalo onwards)</h2>
                    <p class="text-lg text-gray-700">An exhaustive breakdown of Xhosa governance, law, and the decentralization of power.</p>
                </header>

                <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200 mb-8">
                     <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-xhosa-clay mb-4">Chronology of Key Reigns</h3>
                     <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 mb-4">Visualizing the overlapping tenures of paramounts, regents, and Right-Hand house leaders.</p>
                     <div class="chart-container">
                         <canvas id="timelineChart"></canvas>
                     </div>
                </div>

                <div class="space-y-4">
                    <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-lg bg-white shadow-sm">
                        <button class="accordion-toggle w-full px-6 py-4 text-left font-semibold text-xhosa-earth hover:bg-xhosa-sand flex justify-between items-center" onclick="toggleAccordion(this)">
                            Exhaustive Analysis: The Power of the Chief (Inkosi)
                            <span class="text-xl">&#43;</span>
                        </button>
                        <div class="accordion-content px-6 bg-white">
                            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 py-4 space-y-2 text-gray-700">
                                <li><strong>Nature of Authority:</strong> The Chief was not an autocrat. His power was derived from consensus and the support of his councillors (amaphakathi). "A chief is a chief by grace of his people."</li>
                                <li><strong>Prerogatives of the Chief:</strong>
                                    <ul class="list-[circle] pl-6 mt-1 space-y-1">
                                        <li>Receiving tribute (izizi - death duties, fines for shedding blood).</li>
                                        <li>Commanding the army in national conflicts.</li>
                                        <li>Allocating land.</li>
                                        <li>Right of 'umkhosi' (calling the nation to assembly).</li>
                                    </ul>
                                </li>
                                <li><strong>Limitations on Power:</strong>
                                    <ul class="list-[circle] pl-6 mt-1 space-y-1">
                                        <li><strong>The Amaphakathi (Councillors):</strong> Powerful commoners who represented public opinion. A chief could not easily act against their united will.</li>
                                        <li><strong>Fission:</strong> Disgruntled subjects could simply move away and 'khonza' (give allegiance to) a rival chief or a junior son setting up his own chiefdom. This constant threat of emigration kept chiefs responsive to their people.</li>
                                    </ul>
                                </li>
                                <li><strong>The Court (Inkundla):</strong> The center of political and judicial life. All adult men could attend and speak. Justice was restorative rather than purely punitive.</li>
                                <li><strong>The Paramountcy:</strong> The Gcaleka chief (e.g., Hintsa) was Paramount over all Xhosa. However, this was largely ritual and prestige-based. He could not dictate internal policy to the Rharhabe or Ndlambe chiefs.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    
                    <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-lg bg-white shadow-sm">
                        <button class="accordion-toggle w-full px-6 py-4 text-left font-semibold text-xhosa-earth hover:bg-xhosa-sand flex justify-between items-center" onclick="toggleAccordion(this)">
                            Exhaustive Analysis: Law & Society
                            <span class="text-xl">&#43;</span>
                        </button>
                        <div class="accordion-content px-6 bg-white">
                            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 py-4 space-y-2 text-gray-700">
                                <li><strong>Legal Principles:</strong> Customary law was fluid and based on precedent, but adaptable. The primary goal was restoring social harmony and compensation for loss.</li>
                                <li><strong>Property:</strong> Land was communally held by the chief in trust for the nation. Individuals had rights of usage (usufruct), not absolute ownership. Cattle were privately owned.</li>
                                <li><strong>Crimes vs. Civil Wrongs:</strong>
                                    <ul class="list-[circle] pl-6 mt-1 space-y-1">
                                        <li>Treason, witchcraft, and murder were crimes against the Chief (as he owned the blood of his subjects). Fines went to the Chief.</li>
                                        <li>Theft, adultery, and damage to property were civil matters settled by compensation (usually in cattle) to the victim's family.</li>
                                    </ul>
                                </li>
                                <li><strong>Witchcraft (Ubuthakathi):</strong> Used as both a mechanism for social control and a political weapon. Chiefs could use witch-doctors (amagqirha) to eliminate wealthy or overly ambitious rivals by "smelling them out".</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- VIEW: ECONOMY -->
            <section id="view-economy" class="view-section hidden">
                <header class="mb-8 border-b-2 border-xhosa-sand pb-4">
                    <h2 class="text-4xl font-serif text-xhosa-earth font-bold mb-3">Economic Development to 1835</h2>
                    <p class="text-lg text-gray-700">An exhaustive examination of the economic pillars of Xhosa society: cattle, agriculture, and expanding trade networks.</p>
                </header>

                <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 mb-8">
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200 flex flex-col justify-center">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-xhosa-clay mb-2 text-center">Economic Pillars (Estimated Impact)</h3>
                        <p class="text-sm text-center text-gray-500 mb-4">Relative cultural and subsistence value.</p>
                        <div class="chart-container h-[300px] max-h-[300px]">
                            <canvas id="economyChart"></canvas>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-xhosa-leaf mb-4">The Centrality of Cattle</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Cattle were not merely food; they were the foundation of the political, social, and economic system.</p>
                        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-sm text-gray-700">
                            <li><strong>Currency & Wealth:</strong> Used for all major transactions, fines, and storing wealth.</li>
                            <li><strong>Social Reproduction (Lobola):</strong> Bridewealth paid in cattle legitimized marriage and transferred rights over children to the husband's lineage.</li>
                            <li><strong>Political Power (Ubusoqa):</strong> Chiefs loaned cattle to poor commoners. The recipient retained the milk and some offspring, but owed political allegiance and labor to the chief.</li>
                            <li><strong>Religious Significance:</strong> Crucial for sacrifices to ancestors (amathongo).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="space-y-4">
                    <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-lg bg-white shadow-sm">
                        <button class="accordion-toggle w-full px-6 py-4 text-left font-semibold text-xhosa-earth hover:bg-xhosa-sand flex justify-between items-center" onclick="toggleAccordion(this)">
                            Exhaustive Analysis: Agriculture, Hunting & Trade
                            <span class="text-xl">&#43;</span>
                        </button>
                        <div class="accordion-content px-6 bg-white">
                            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 py-4 space-y-2 text-gray-700">
                                <li><strong>Agriculture:</strong>
                                    <ul class="list-[circle] pl-6 mt-1 space-y-1">
                                        <li>Sorghum, maize (introduced later), pumpkins, melons, and beans.</li>
                                        <li>Women performed the majority of agricultural labor.</li>
                                        <li>Vulnerable to drought and locusts, making the reliability of cattle even more critical.</li>
                                    </ul>
                                </li>
                                <li><strong>Hunting:</strong> Primarily a male activity. Provided dietary supplement but also crucial trade goods (ivory, skins) for external markets.</li>
                                <li><strong>Pre-Colonial Trade:</strong>
                                    <ul class="list-[circle] pl-6 mt-1 space-y-1">
                                        <li>Traded tobacco and cannabis (dagga) with Khoisan in exchange for cattle and clientship.</li>
                                        <li>Traded iron and copper with groups to the East (Thembu, Mpondo).</li>
                                    </ul>
                                </li>
                                <li><strong>Trade with the Cape Colony:</strong>
                                    <ul class="list-[circle] pl-6 mt-1 space-y-1">
                                        <li>Initially intermittent (ivory/skins for beads, copper, iron).</li>
                                        <li>Post-1820: Establishment of formal trade fairs (e.g., Fort Willshire).</li>
                                        <li><strong>Impact:</strong> Introduction of European goods (blankets, iron hoes, beads) shifted internal economic dynamics. Chiefs tried to monopolize trade to reinforce their power, but commoners also accumulated wealth.</li>
                                        <li>Creation of a new class of intermediaries and increased demand for colonial goods.</li>
                                    </ul>
                                </li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
