            <!-- VIEW: CONFLICTS -->
            <section id="view-conflicts" class="view-section hidden">
                <header class="mb-8 border-b-2 border-xhosa-sand pb-4">
                    <h2 class="text-4xl font-serif text-xhosa-earth font-bold mb-3">Frontier Dynamics: From Ngqika's Peace to Maqoma's War</h2>
                    <p class="text-lg text-gray-700">An exhaustive chronological and structural breakdown of the pressures that led to the Frontier Wars (up to 1835).</p>
                </header>

                <div class="bg-xhosa-sand p-6 rounded-lg mb-8 border-l-4 border-xhosa-clay">
                    <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-xhosa-earth mb-2">The Core Misunderstanding</h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-800">The colonial government demanded a fixed, impermeable border (the Fish River, later Keiskamma) and a single autocratic "King" (Ngqika) to hold responsible for all cattle thefts. The Xhosa political system was decentralized, expansive, and borders were viewed as porous zones of interaction, not rigid lines.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="space-y-4">
                    <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-lg bg-white shadow-sm">
                        <button class="accordion-toggle w-full px-6 py-4 text-left font-semibold text-xhosa-earth hover:bg-xhosa-sand flex justify-between items-center" onclick="toggleAccordion(this)">
                            Exhaustive Timeline: The Zuurveld & The Rharhabe Schism (1770s - 1819)
                            <span class="text-xl">&#43;</span>
                        </button>
                        <div class="accordion-content px-6 bg-white">
                            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 py-4 space-y-2 text-gray-700">
                                <li><strong>The Zuurveld Contest:</strong> The area between the Sundays and Fish rivers was prime grazing land contested by Trekboers, Khoikhoi, and Xhosa (specifically Ndlambe and Gqunukhwebe).</li>
                                <li><strong>The Ngqika-Ndlambe Rivalry:</strong>
                                    <ul class="list-[circle] pl-6 mt-1 space-y-1">
                                        <li>Ndlambe acted as regent for the young Ngqika. When Ngqika came of age, Ndlambe refused to relinquish total control.</li>
                                        <li>This led to a bitter, decades-long civil war within the Rharhabe branch.</li>
                                    </ul>
                                </li>
                                <li><strong>Colonial Intervention (1811-1812):</strong> The Fourth Frontier War. Lt. Col. John Graham brutally expelled Ndlambe and 20,000 Xhosa from the Zuurveld across the Fish River, causing massive overcrowding.</li>
                                <li><strong>The Somerset-Ngqika Treaty (1817):</strong> Governor Lord Charles Somerset recognized Ngqika as the supreme Xhosa monarch (a fiction) and instituted the "Spooor Law" (allowing commandos to trace stolen cattle to the nearest kraal and extract compensation).</li>
                                <li><strong>Battle of Amalinde (1818):</strong> Ndlambe, supported by the prophet Nxele (Makhanda) and Hintsa, decisively defeated Ngqika's forces.</li>
                                <li><strong>Fifth Frontier War (1819):</strong> Ngqika appealed to the British. British forces repelled Ndlambe's attack on Grahamstown (led by Nxele). As "punishment", Somerset annexed the land between the Fish and Keiskamma rivers from *all* Xhosa (including his ally Ngqika) to create a "Neutral Territory".</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-lg bg-white shadow-sm">
                        <button class="accordion-toggle w-full px-6 py-4 text-left font-semibold text-xhosa-earth hover:bg-xhosa-sand flex justify-between items-center" onclick="toggleAccordion(this)">
                            Exhaustive Timeline: The Rise of Maqoma & The 1835 War (1820 - 1835)
                            <span class="text-xl">&#43;</span>
                        </button>
                        <div class="accordion-content px-6 bg-white">
                            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 py-4 space-y-2 text-gray-700">
                                <li><strong>1820 Settlers:</strong> Introduction of 4,000 British settlers into the Zuurveld (Albany) closed the frontier entirely and intensified land pressure.</li>
                                <li><strong>Maqoma (Ngqika's Right-Hand Son):</strong> Emerged as the most brilliant political and military leader of his generation. He established himself in the Kat River valley (part of the "Neutral" territory).</li>
                                <li><strong>The Expulsions:</strong>
                                    <ul class="list-[circle] pl-6 mt-1 space-y-1">
                                        <li>1829: Maqoma expelled from the Kat River Valley to make way for a Khoikhoi settlement.</li>
                                        <li>1833: Tyali (Ngqika's minor son) and Maqoma repeatedly expelled from the Tyhume valley.</li>
                                        <li>The constant military patrols, burning of huts, and seizing of cattle under the reprisal system caused immense hardship and anger.</li>
                                    </ul>
                                </li>
                                <li><strong>The Sixth Frontier War (1834-1835) - Hintsa's War:</strong>
                                    <ul class="list-[circle] pl-6 mt-1 space-y-1">
                                        <li>Driven to desperation by land hunger and colonial harassment, Maqoma and Tyali invaded the colony in December 1834.</li>
                                        <li>Governor D'Urban retaliated massively.</li>
                                        <li><strong>The Death of Hintsa (1835):</strong> Though Hintsa (the Gcaleka Paramount) tried to remain neutral, D'Urban held him responsible for the actions of the Rharhabe. Hintsa was taken hostage, mutilated, and murdered by colonial troops while allegedly trying to escape. This event left a permanent scar on Xhosa-colonial relations.</li>
                                    </ul>
                                </li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- VIEW: RELIGION -->
            <section id="view-religion" class="view-section hidden">
                <header class="mb-8 border-b-2 border-xhosa-sand pb-4">
                    <h2 class="text-4xl font-serif text-xhosa-earth font-bold mb-3">Religion & The Impact of Christianity</h2>
                    <p class="text-lg text-gray-700">An exhaustive comparison of traditional Xhosa cosmology and the disruptive/transformative effects of early missionaries.</p>
                </header>

                <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
                    <!-- Traditional Religion -->
                    <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-lg shadow-sm p-6">
                        <h3 class="text-2xl font-serif text-xhosa-earth border-b-2 border-xhosa-sand pb-2 mb-4">Traditional Xhosa Religion</h3>
                        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-3 text-gray-700">
                            <li><strong>Supreme Being (Qamata/Thixo):</strong> A distant creator god, rarely approached directly.</li>
                            <li><strong>The Ancestors (Amathongo):</strong> The active spiritual agents. They concerned themselves with the welfare of their living descendants.</li>
                            <li><strong>Ritual & Sacrifice:</strong> Harmony with ancestors was maintained through custom and sacrifices (usually cattle) at critical life stages (birth, initiation, marriage, death) or during illness.</li>
                            <li><strong>Pollution (Umlaza):</strong> Ritual impurity caused by menstruation, childbirth, or contact with corpses, requiring purification.</li>
                            <li><strong>Diviners (Amagqirha):</strong> Called by the ancestors to diagnose illness, find lost objects, and "smell out" witches. They were the intellectual and spiritual leaders.</li>
                            <li><strong>Cosmology:</strong> Religion was deeply integrated into the social and political fabric. It explained misfortune and provided methods for restoring order.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Christian Impact -->
                    <div class="bg-xhosa-sand border border-xhosa-clay rounded-lg shadow-sm p-6">
                        <h3 class="text-2xl font-serif text-xhosa-clay border-b-2 border-white pb-2 mb-4">The Missionary Impact</h3>
                        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-3 text-gray-800">
                            <li><strong>Early Agents:</strong> J.T. van der Kemp (London Missionary Society), followed by John Brownlee, William Shaw (Wesleyan).</li>
                            <li><strong>Subversion of Custom:</strong> Missionaries attacked the core of Xhosa society:
                                <ul class="list-[square] pl-6 mt-1 space-y-1 text-sm">
                                    <li>Condemned Lobola (bridewealth) as 'buying wives'.</li>
                                    <li>Condemned initiation rites and polygyny.</li>
                                    <li>Undermined the authority of chiefs and amagqirha.</li>
                                </ul>
                            </li>
                            <li><strong>Creation of the 'School People' (Amagqobhoka):</strong> Converts often moved to mission stations, severing ties with their chiefs and traditional obligations, creating a divided society (Red vs. School).</li>
                            <li><strong>The Prophetic Response:</strong>
                                <ul class="list-[square] pl-6 mt-1 space-y-1 text-sm">
                                    <li>Figures like <strong>Nxele</strong> (Makhanda) and <strong>Ntsikana</strong> synthesized Christian concepts with Xhosa worldview.</li>
                                    <li>Nxele developed a militant theology of a Black God (Mdalidiphu) combating the White God, leading the 1819 attack on Grahamstown.</li>
                                    <li>Ntsikana preached submission and peace, creating the first great Xhosa Christian hymns.</li>
                                </ul>
                            </li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

        </div>
    </main>

    <!-- Vanilla JS Logic -->
    <script>
        // --- Navigation Logic ---
        function switchTab(tabId) {
            // Update buttons
            document.querySelectorAll('.nav-btn').forEach(btn => {
                btn.classList.remove('active');
                if(btn.dataset.target === tabId) btn.classList.add('active');
            });

            // Update views
            document.querySelectorAll('.view-section').forEach(section => {
                section.classList.remove('block');
                section.classList.add('hidden');
            });
            const targetView = document.getElementById('view-' + tabId);
            targetView.classList.remove('hidden');
            targetView.classList.add('block');

            // Trigger chart renders if they haven't been rendered yet or need resizing
            if(tabId === 'political') initTimelineChart();
            if(tabId === 'economy') initEconomyChart();
            
            // Scroll to top of main content
            document.querySelector('main').scrollTop = 0;
        }

        // --- Accordion Logic ---
        function toggleAccordion(button) {
            const content = button.nextElementSibling;
            const icon = button.querySelector('span');
            
            if (content.classList.contains('open')) {
                content.classList.remove('open');
                button.classList.remove('bg-xhosa-sand');
                icon.innerHTML = '&#43;'; // Plus
            } else {
                content.classList.add('open');
                button.classList.add('bg-xhosa-sand');
                icon.innerHTML = '&#8722;'; // Minus
            }
        }

        // --- Interactive Lineage Data & Rendering ---
        const lineageData = {
            name: "Xhosa (Mythical Founder)",
            desc: "The eponymous ancestor. Real political unification began later.",
            children: [
                {
                    name: "Tshawe (The Usurper & Unifier)",
                    desc: "Overthrew his senior brother Cirha with the amaRudulu. Established the royal clan and the mechanism of ukukhonza (clientage) to incorporate alien groups.",
                    children: [
                        {
                            name: "Phalo (c. 1715 - 1775)",
                            desc: "His reign saw the structural division of the nation into two Great Houses to prevent civil war over his two equal-ranked wives.",
                            children: [
                                {
                                    name: "Gcaleka (Great House - East of Kei)",
                                    desc: "Paramount in rank, but lacked direct political control over the Rharhabe. Attempted to consolidate power by becoming an igqirha (diviner).",
                                    children: [
                                        {
                                            name: "Khawuta",
                                            desc: "A weak ruler. His reign saw the consolidation of Rharhabe independence.",
                                            children: [
                                                {
                                                    name: "Hintsa (c. 1804 - 1835)",
                                                    desc: "Paramount during the escalating frontier conflicts. Attempted neutrality but was drawn into the 1835 war, taken hostage by D'Urban, and murdered. A pivotal trauma in Xhosa history."
                                                }
                                            ]
                                        }
                                    ]
                                },
                                {
                                    name: "Rharhabe (Right-Hand House - West of Kei)",
                                    desc: "Politically independent, expansive, and militaristic. Conquered Khoisan and Thembu territory. Died in battle against the Thembu.",
                                    children: [
                                        {
                                            name: "Mlawu (Died before ruling)",
                                            desc: "Heir of Rharhabe, died young, leaving a minor son (Ngqika).",
                                            children: [
                                                {
                                                    name: "Ngqika (1795 - 1829)",
                                                    desc: "Allied with the British to fight his uncle Ndlambe. Falsely recognized as 'King of the Xhosa' by Somerset. Yielded the Neutral Territory, losing legitimacy among his people.",
                                                    children: [
                                                        { name: "Sandile (Great Son)", desc: "Minor at Ngqika's death." },
                                                        { name: "Maqoma (Right-Hand Son)", desc: "The brilliant military tactician and regent. Fought relentlessly against colonial expulsion from the Kat River and Tyhume valleys. Primary leader in the 1835 war." },
                                                        { name: "Tyali", desc: "Key leader in the 1835 war alongside Maqoma." }
                                                    ]
                                                }
                                            ]
                                        },
                                        {
                                            name: "Ndlambe (Regent)",
                                            desc: "Regent for Ngqika who refused to yield power. Built a massive following. Defeated Ngqika at Amalinde (1818) but was broken by British forces in 1819."
                                        }
                                    ]
                                }
                            ]
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ]
        };

        function buildLineageHTML(node) {
            let html = `<li class="ml-4 mt-2 border-l-2 border-xhosa-sand pl-4">
                <button class="text-left font-semibold text-xhosa-earth hover:text-xhosa-clay focus:outline-none flex items-center" onclick="toggleLineageNode(this)">
                    <span class="mr-2 text-xs">&#9654;</span> ${node.name}
                </button>
                <div class="text-sm text-gray-600 mt-1 mb-2 hidden lineage-desc bg-gray-50 p-2 rounded">${node.desc}</div>
            `;
            
            if (node.children && node.children.length > 0) {
                html += `<ul class="hidden lineage-children">`;
                node.children.forEach(child => {
                    html += buildLineageHTML(child);
                });
                html += `</ul>`;
            }
            html += `</li>`;
            return html;
        }

        function toggleLineageNode(btn) {
            const icon = btn.querySelector('span');
            const desc = btn.nextElementSibling;
            const childrenList = desc.nextElementSibling;
            
            if (desc.classList.contains('hidden')) {
                desc.classList.remove('hidden');
                icon.innerHTML = '&#9660;'; // Down arrow
                if (childrenList) childrenList.classList.remove('hidden');
            } else {
                desc.classList.add('hidden');
                icon.innerHTML = '&#9654;'; // Right arrow
                if (childrenList) childrenList.classList.add('hidden');
            }
        }

        // Initialize Lineage
        document.getElementById('lineage-root').innerHTML = buildLineageHTML(lineageData);
        // Auto-expand the first two levels
        const firstBtn = document.querySelector('#lineage-root > li > button');
        if(firstBtn) toggleLineageNode(firstBtn);
        const secondBtn = document.querySelector('#lineage-root > li > ul > li > button');
        if(secondBtn) toggleLineageNode(secondBtn);

        // --- Chart.js Initializations ---
        let timelineChartInstance = null;
        let economyChartInstance = null;

        function initTimelineChart() {
            if(timelineChartInstance) return; // Only init once
            
            const ctx = document.getElementById('timelineChart').getContext('2d');
            
            // Re-formatting data for a horizontal bar to simulate a Gantt/Timeline
            const data = {
                labels: ['Phalo (Unified)', 'Gcaleka (Great House)', 'Rharhabe (Right-Hand)', 'Ndlambe (Regent)', 'Ngqika', 'Hintsa (Paramount)'],
                datasets: [{
                    label: 'Reign Duration',
                    // Format: [Start Year, End Year]
                    data: [
                        [1715, 1775],
                        [1750, 1778],
                        [1750, 1782],
                        [1782, 1819], // End of massive power post 5th war
                        [1795, 1829],
                        [1804, 1835]
                    ],
                    backgroundColor: [
                        '#B45309', '#78350F', '#064E3B', '#1C1917', '#B45309', '#78350F'
                    ],
                    borderWidth: 1,
                    borderRadius: 4
                }]
            };

            timelineChartInstance = new Chart(ctx, {
                type: 'bar',
                data: data,
                options: {
                    indexAxis: 'y', // Horizontal
                    responsive: true,
                    maintainAspectRatio: false,
                    plugins: {
                        legend: { display: false },
                        tooltip: {
                            callbacks: {
                                label: function(context) {
                                    return `Reign: ${context.raw[0]} - ${context.raw[1]}`;
                                }
                            }
                        }
                    },
                    scales: {
                        x: {
                            min: 1700,
                            max: 1840,
                            title: { display: true, text: 'Year' }
                        }
                    }
                }
            });
        }

        function initEconomyChart() {
            if(economyChartInstance) return;
            
            const ctx = document.getElementById('economyChart').getContext('2d');
            
            economyChartInstance = new Chart(ctx, {
                type: 'doughnut',
                data: {
                    labels: ['Cattle (Wealth/Status/Food)', 'Agriculture (Sorghum/Maize)', 'Hunting & Foraging', 'External Trade (Ivory/Skins)'],
                    datasets: [{
                        data: [50, 30, 10, 10], // Estimated relative importance based on text
                        backgroundColor: [
                            '#78350F', // Earth (Cattle)
                            '#064E3B', // Leaf (Agri)
                            '#B45309', // Clay (Hunting)
                            '#FEF3C7'  // Sand (Trade)
                        ],
                        borderColor: '#FAFAF9',
                        borderWidth: 2
                    }]
                },
                options: {
                    responsive: true,
                    maintainAspectRatio: false,
                    plugins: {
                        legend: {
                            position: 'bottom',
                            labels: {
                                font: { family: "'Inter', sans-serif" },
                                boxWidth: 12
                            }
                        },
                        tooltip: {
                            callbacks: {
                                label: function(context) {
                                    // Wrap label if too long (16 char logic requested, though standard Chart.js handles this okay, we force succinctness)
                                    let label = context.label || '';
                                    if(label.length > 20) label = label.substring(0, 20) + '...';
                                    return ` ${label}: ~${context.raw}% impact`;
                                }
                            }
                        }
                    },
                    cutout: '60%'
                }
            });
        }
    </script>
</body>
</html>
